  "Osaka Nikkei Miami": {
    "placeId": "ChIJiXRJqIG22YgRiV_m6a33YE4",
    "address": "1300 Brickell Bay Dr, Miami, FL 33131, USA",
    "lat": 25.7601507,
    "lng": -80.1906071,
    "rating": 4.5,
    "userRatingsTotal": 816,
    "phone": "(786) 627-4800",
    "website": "https://osakanikkei.com/miami/",
    "priceLevel": 4,
    "openingHours": [
      "Monday: 6:00 – 11:00 PM",
      "Tuesday: 12:00 – 2:30 PM, 6:00 – 11:00 PM",
      "Wednesday: 12:00 – 2:30 PM, 6:00 – 11:00 PM",
      "Thursday: 12:00 – 2:30 PM, 6:00 – 11:00 PM",
      "Friday: 12:00 – 2:30 PM, 6:00 PM – 12:00 AM",
      "Saturday: 6:00 PM – 12:00 AM",
      "Sunday: 12:00 – 4:00 PM, 6:00 – 10:00 PM"
    ],
    "mapsUrl": "https://maps.google.com/?cid=5647786259048193929",
    "reviews": [
      {
        "author": "Robert Muffaletto",
        "authorPhoto": "https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLiA18zsPAQ4-htHshoaj53BvgTekJEd5edqAsPC-J2yANrSw=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba5",
        "rating": 5,
        "relativeTime": "3 months ago",
        "text": "Food:\nOsaka was excellent. The meal had a lot of variety, but it never felt scattered. The raw dishes were a great place to start because the quality was obvious right away. The fish tasted fresh, clean, and carefully handled, and even the more composed bites still let that come through. Nothing felt dressed up just for the sake of it. The richer plates were just as strong. The wagyu had a lot of flavor and real depth, but it still fit the meal instead of pulling it in a completely different direction. The rice dishes stood out too. Flavorful, substantial, and satisfying without feeling too heavy. That was probably my favorite part of eating here. There was a lot going on, but the kitchen kept control of it. Every dish felt like it belonged.\n\nService:\nPolished and attentive. The pacing of the meal was smooth, and everything came out in a way that let you enjoy each dish without feeling rushed.\n\nSummary:\nEasy 5/5. Osaka felt thoughtful, exciting, and very well executed. The meal had range, but the quality stayed consistent the whole way through."
      },
      {
        "author": "Frank Garcia",
        "authorPhoto": "https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjXGfePFisYnRD9-OlgVxHYB8S-f4wfcSxsTanwqaHxnyEv3-O1T=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba6",
        "rating": 4,
        "relativeTime": "4 months ago",
        "text": "I had been to their Lima location about a year ago during a trip to Peru and absolutely loved the flavors, so I was curious to see how it would compare to their Brickell location back home.\n\nOverall, the flavors were very consistent with what I remember from Lima, which was great to see. The main differences, however, came down to portion sizes and pricing, understandably higher in the U.S., but still noticeable.\n\nI ordered mostly the same dishes I had in Lima, and the Carpassion was the standout of the night. The combination of flavors with that touch of sweetness was just delicious. That said, the slices were noticeably thinner compared to what I had in Peru, I even compared photos, and the difference was pretty significant. I understand operating costs vary, but I do think there should be a bit more consistency across locations.\n\nMy second favorite was the Osk (tuna ceviche). The Asian-inspired flavors really came through here, balanced, vibrant, and honestly just excellent.\n\nThe Kiiro, their take on traditional ceviche, delivered exactly what you’d expect, clean, authentic, and well executed.\n\nThe only miss for me was the Nori Furai. Some pieces had a noticeably fishier taste compared to others, which threw things off a bit. It could have been the fish itself or maybe the sauce, but it wasn’t as consistent as the rest of the meal.\n\nCocktails were solid, but definitely on the expensive side at around $24 each.\n\nAll in all, it was a good dinner with strong flavors and a nice reminder of my experience in Lima. That said, I’m not entirely sure it justifies the price point, but still a spot worth trying."
      },
      {
        "author": "Keith Darby",
        "authorPhoto": "https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjU7h7N9rkJ1_hKcS1ICXHbegCGIIVbEZb7gXsHN4jMzKxFyZ_igeg=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba2",
        "rating": 5,
        "relativeTime": "4 months ago",
        "text": "⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Osaka Miami — A Business Lunch That Ruined Every Other Sushi Spot\nWe came for a business lunch and left completely recalibrated on what Japanese cuisine in Miami can be. Osaka has quietly become one of the best Japanese restaurants in the country, and after today, I'd argue it's not even close.\nWe started with what our table immediately crowned the star of the meal — the salmon carpaccio (or as I was apparently shouting by the end: CARPASSION). Sliced in a dramatic sunburst arrangement and dressed in a citrusy, vibrant sauce with crispy garnish and microgreens, it was as beautiful as it was exceptional. We ordered two plates and would have ordered a third. It's the kind of dish you'll be thinking about for days.\nThe tuna nigiri followed — elegant, precisely seasoned, topped with crispy aromatics and scallion. Simple, but the quality of the fish spoke for itself. And the crispy rice bites topped with what appeared to be spiced tuna and truffle shavings were an absolute standout — light, crunchy, rich, and completely addictive.\nThe space itself is gorgeous — warm wood tones, bamboo-lined windows, impeccably set tables. The vibe is elevated without being stiff, which made it ideal for a business lunch that didn't feel like a business lunch.\nThe real kicker? At roughly $112/person with a substantial amount of food, the value for this level of quality is almost unreasonable. This is essentially what Uchi charges for happy hour — minus the drinks — and Osaka delivers a full, serious meal for it. For Miami, that's remarkable.\nTo anyone who had Uchi on their shortlist: 🦗🦗🦗\nOsaka Miami is not just the best Japanese restaurant in Miami — it's one of the best in the country. Full stop."
      }
    ]
  },
