  "Nami Nori": {
    "placeId": "ChIJ83kXEMyx2YgRldpEPueN0B0",
    "address": "156 NE 41st St, Miami, FL 33137, USA",
    "lat": 25.8139927,
    "lng": -80.1921594,
    "rating": 4.6,
    "userRatingsTotal": 554,
    "phone": "(786) 598-8848",
    "website": "http://naminori.us/",
    "priceLevel": 3,
    "openingHours": [
      "Monday: 12:00 – 10:00 PM",
      "Tuesday: 12:00 – 10:00 PM",
      "Wednesday: 12:00 – 10:00 PM",
      "Thursday: 12:00 – 10:00 PM",
      "Friday: 12:00 – 10:00 PM",
      "Saturday: 12:00 – 10:00 PM",
      "Sunday: 12:00 – 9:00 PM"
    ],
    "mapsUrl": "https://maps.google.com/?cid=2148373046577388181",
    "reviews": [
      {
        "author": "na nguyen",
        "authorPhoto": "https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocJOWZKQsoUUmHSXUEbJZXn2DnD0uexkE2jOg7pdVGRI3ezqY-k=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba3",
        "rating": 5,
        "relativeTime": "8 months ago",
        "text": "Unique hand roll place. Got to tried the signature set with the furikake fries. The fries was pretty mid imo. The best roll I think is coconut shrimp and x.o scallops. The coconut is really subtle, not too strong but still gives you a nice coconut touch to it. The x.o sauce with the scallops was pretty unique, the salmon Serrano was good too but nothing stood out- same with the tuna.\np/s: they mix the wasabi with rice so just tell them beforehand your preference.\noverall: 9/10"
      },
      {
        "author": "Lee",
        "authorPhoto": "https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjXx0hm4Thn9acmK-8O_64hIEOQ4MOZisjhaVtVBoHmCn5j7R6w=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba5",
        "rating": 5,
        "relativeTime": "2 months ago",
        "text": "⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐\nNami Nori in the Design District is simply one of the best Japanese dining experiences in Miami — and I've eaten at a lot of sushi spots. This one stands apart.\nThe concept is brilliant — hand rolls assembled at the table with fresh nori wraps, but what really sets it apart is the execution. Every single dish arrived with its own distinct personality, each variation subtle enough to enhance rather than overwhelm. That restraint is the mark of a truly skilled kitchen.\nThe tuna crudo was delicate and beautifully balanced. The trout sashimi with its citrus notes was outstanding. The wasabi cucumber tataki — simple but perfectly executed. The a la carte selections — tuna poke, salmon serrano, spicy lobster with crunchy nori, miso cod — each one a highlight. The coconut shrimp add-on was a genius touch.\nEven the miso chocolate chip cookies to finish were a perfect little sendoff.\nThe space is clean, minimal and elegant — the Design District setting suits it perfectly. Service from Daiana was attentive and knowledgeable throughout.\nAt $227 for two, it's a splurge — but every dollar is justified. This is Japanese food executed at the highest level. An absolute must visit. 🍣🖤"
      },
      {
        "author": "Elise Burnett",
        "authorPhoto": "https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocL4i81OqvEjQm4-KxvIZJa1y3Q2103Yt9Sp2WFz3RHfhSkf8A=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo",
        "rating": 5,
        "relativeTime": "6 months ago",
        "text": "I had an excellent experience here. I appreciate that the entire menu is gluten free. I have celiac disease, so it’s nice not to worry about cross contamination of any kind. I also have several other ingredient intolerances and Stephon was incredibly knowledgeable and attentive when helping me navigate the menu. I appreciate being able to have a meal and not worry about getting sick after, which seems like a low standard, but isn’t when you’re navigating multiple intolerances. I didn’t love that dogs are allowed on the patio, and the inside of the restaurant is quite small and can be hard for the staff and the patrons to navigate at times. I loved my meal and the overall experience and I will definitely return when I’m back in town."
      }
    ]
  },
